<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>What does the data tell us, if anything, about the current population in the cities or what the population will be in 2050?</item> <item> How would you describe the population change in each city during this time period? Write one to two sentences for each city. Then discuss with your group. </item> <item> What kind of model (linear, exponential, both, or neither) do you think is appropriate for each city's population? </item> <item> For each population that you think can be modeled by a linear and/or exponential function: </item> <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
    <item> Write an equation for the function(s). </item> <item> Graph the function(s). </item> </list>
  <item> Compare the graphs of your functions with the actual population data. How well do the models fit the data? </item> <item> Use your models to predict the population in each city in 2010, the current year, and 2050. </item> <item> Do you think that these predictions are (or will be) accurate? Be prepared to show your reasoning. </item>
</list>
<title>Student Response</title>

<list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
  <item>For example: The data tells us the trend in population change in the three cities between 1950 and 2000. These trends may or may not persist. So the data allows us to make an informed prediction for the population in 2010, 2050, or today but the predictions may not be accurate if conditions in the cities have changed. See the answers to 5 below for some estimates. </item>
  <item>For example: 
    <list>
      <item> The Paris population is increasing but looks to increase more and more slowly as time goes on.  </item>
      <item> The Austin population is increasing more and more rapidly as time goes on.  </item>
      <item> The Chicago population is decreasing until the decade from 1990 to 2000 when it increases. The rate of decrease from 1950 through 1990 is not constant.  </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>For example: 
    <list>
      <item> For Paris, a linear model might be appropriate. The successive differences for the populations are 1,100,000, 800,000, 500,000, 600,000, 400,000. While these are not constant, they show the same general trend upward at a little more than 500,000 per year. This variation in differences is large enough that some may decide a linear model is not appropriate. An exponential model for Paris could also be used with a rate of growth, per decade, at about 1.07. This seems less appropriate than the linear model, however, because the amount of growth each decade is not accelerating (which we would expect if it were growing exponentially).  </item>
      <item> For Austin, a linear model is not appropriate because the successive differences are steadily increasing. An exponential model would be appropriate because the successive population quotients, while not all the same, are in a pretty narrow range between 1.36 and 1.42.  </item>
      <item> For Chicago, the population is decreasing for most of the period, but then it increases slightly at the end. The successive differences in the population are not that close to being constant so a linear model is not the best—we could draw a line connecting the initial and final populations but this overestimates the population for much of the time between 1950 and 2000. An exponentially decreasing model is also not appropriate—the successive quotients are not far from 1, but this will also fail to capture the growth at the end.  </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>For example: <list class="os-raise-noindent" type="enumerated">
      <item/>
    </list>
    <list>
      <item> For Paris, the population (in millions) \(d\) decades after 1950, is modeled by \(f\) where \(f(d)=0.7d+6.3\). </item>
      <item> For Austin, the population (in hundred thousands) \(d\) decades after 1950, is modeled by \(g\) where \(g(d)=1.32(1.38)^d\). </item>
      <item> For Chicago, no good linear or exponential model.  </item>
    </list>
    <list class="os-raise-noindent" start="2" type="enumerated">
      <item>Paris:<figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/61fcd6b4f3526b59b90622071e563df2b8ade98e"/></media></figure><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4e9dc7637967f10d4e2e8ee1dd58c2375009abc4"/></media></figure><figure><media alt="Insert Alt Text"><image mime-type="image/png" src="https://k12.openstax.org/contents/raise/resources/4ac4d2d810aaca3522e7eecb5eeace68be7030fc"/></media></figure></item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item>For example: <list>
      <item> The model for the Paris data fits ok but it's not perfect. While it is not too far off for any of the given decades, there is a trend that indicates that the linear model is probably not the best—the line consistently underestimates the population for earlier decades and then overestimates the population at the very end. This trend in the residuals indicates that a different model would probably be better.  </item>
      <item> The model for Austin fits the data very well. In fact, it is difficult to tell that the variation in successive quotients for the population each decade is significant (from about 1.36 to about 1.42). Maybe since the graph is only for 5 decades, these differences are not substantial enough to be visible.  </item>
      <item> For Chicago, we can see that the data does not have an exponential shape. We could use a horizontal line, indicating a constant population of about 3,000,000. But while this is never too far off from any of the given data points, it misses the overall trend in the population change.  </item>
    </list>
  </item>
  <item> For example: <para>Using \(d = 6\) to find the population in 2010 and \(d = 7.2\) to find the population in 2022 and \(d = 10\) to find the population in 2050. For Chicago, there is no good linear or exponential model to find the population beyond the year 2000.</para>
  </item>
